  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—AI systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code—has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful systems introduce significant security and safety concerns that necessitate rigorous evaluation and red-teaming methodologies. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming techniques. We examine the landscape of code generation models, discuss the security risks associated with autonomous code execution, and review state-of-the-art red-teaming frameworks designed to evaluate and mitigate these risks. We analyze key benchmarks including RedCode and RedCodeAgent, evaluate trustworthiness assessment frameworks inspired by DecodingTrust, and discuss the foundational transformer architectures that enable these capabilities. Our work synthesizes current research and identifies critical open challenges in ensuring the safe deployment of code agents in production environments.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how humans interact with software development tools. By leveraging large language models (LLMs) with code execution capabilities, these agents can autonomously generate, test, and debug code across diverse programming contexts [2510.02609]. From automated software engineering to intelligent coding assistants, code agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding requirements, generating implementations, and iterating on solutions.\n\nHowever, the deployment of code agents introduces substantial security and safety challenges. When agents can execute arbitrary code, they become potential attack vectors that could lead to malicious code execution, data exfiltration, or system compromise. The risks extend beyond traditional software vulnerabilities to encompass novel threats arising from AI-driven code generation and execution pipelines.\n\nRed-teaming—systematically probing systems to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent safety. Recent frameworks have been developed to automatically test code agents against various attack vectors, including prompt injection, jailbreaking, and exploitation of code interpreter vulnerabilities [2501.01234]. These red-teaming efforts are essential for understanding the attack surface of code agents and developing appropriate safeguards.\n\nThis paper contributes the following:\n1. A comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and capabilities\n2. An analysis of security risks associated with code agent deployment\n3. A review of red-teaming methodologies and benchmarks for code agents\n4. An examination of trustworthiness assessment frameworks\n5. Identification of open research challenges and future directions\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Generation Models\n\nThe foundation of modern code agents lies in the transformer architecture, which has become the dominant paradigm for sequence modeling tasks [1706.03762]. The attention mechanism introduced in this seminal work enables models to capture long-range dependencies and complex patterns in code, forming the backbone of contemporary code generation systems. Transformer-based models have been extensively adapted for code generation tasks, with architectures specifically designed to handle the structural and semantic nuances of programming languages.\n\n### Red-teaming Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing automated red-teaming frameworks specifically for code agents. The RedCodeAgent framework introduces an automatic red-teaming agent designed to probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities [2510.02609]. This work demonstrates how adversarial techniques can be systematically applied to identify security weaknesses in code agents, including vulnerabilities in code interpretation and execution pipelines. The framework employs iterative refinement of attack strategies to uncover sophisticated vulnerabilities that might elude manual testing.\n\n### Security Benchmarks for Code Agents\n\nComprehensive evaluation benchmarks are essential for assessing code agent safety. The RedCode benchmark provides a systematic framework for evaluating risky code execution and generation in code agents [2501.01234]. This benchmark categorizes security risks across multiple dimensions, including code execution safety, data leakage potential, and malicious code generation. By providing standardized evaluation protocols, RedCode enables systematic comparison of code agent safety across different implementations and configurations.\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment\n\nThe assessment of trustworthiness in AI systems has been an active area of research. The DecodingTrust framework provides a comprehensive assessment methodology for evaluating the trustworthiness of generative models [2306.11698]. While originally developed for general GPT models, its assessment framework has proven valuable for evaluating code agents across multiple trustworthiness dimensions, including robustness, fairness, and transparency. The framework's multi-dimensional approach enables comprehensive evaluation that captures the nuanced trustworthiness requirements of code agents operating in diverse contexts.\n\n## Methodology\n\n### Code Agent Architecture\n\nModern code agents typically consist of the following components:\n\n1. **Language Model Backbone**: Based on transformer architectures that provide contextual understanding and code generation capabilities [1706.03762].\n\n2. **Code Execution Engine**: A sandboxed environment for executing generated code safely, with monitoring capabilities for detecting malicious behavior.\n\n3. **Planning and Reasoning Module**: Enables the agent to decompose complex tasks, plan execution strategies, and iteratively refine solutions.\n\n4. **Safety Filters**: Pre- and post-processing components that validate code inputs and outputs against safety policies.\n\n### Red-teaming Framework\n\nOur red-teaming methodology encompasses multiple attack vectors:\n\n1. **Direct Prompt Attacks**: Adversarial prompts designed to elicit malicious code generation.\n\n2. **Indirect Prompt Attacks**: Subtle manipulations that bypass safety filters through clever phrasing or context manipulation.\n\n3. **Code Interpreter Exploitation**: Attempts to exploit vulnerabilities in the code execution environment.\n\n4. **Multi-turn Attacks**: Sequential interactions designed to gradually bypass safety measures over extended conversations.\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe evaluate code agent safety across the following dimensions:\n\n1. **Vulnerability Detection Rate**: Percentage of known attack vectors successfully identified.\n\n2. **Attack Success Rate**: Rate at which adversarial prompts successfully elicit unsafe behavior.\n\n3. **Robustness Score**: Performance consistency across diverse attack strategies.\n\n4. **False Positive Rate**: Rate of safe code incorrectly flagged as unsafe.\n\n## Results and Discussion\n\n### Code Agent Vulnerabilities\n\nOur analysis reveals that code agents exhibit vulnerabilities across multiple attack vectors. The combination of natural language understanding and code execution capabilities creates a unique attack surface that combines elements of both LLM vulnerabilities and traditional software vulnerabilities.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework [2510.02609] demonstrates that automated red-teaming can identify vulnerabilities that manual testing often misses. Key findings include:\n\n- Most code agents are vulnerable to indirect prompt attacks that manipulate the execution context\n- Code interpretation pipelines present additional attack vectors not present in pure text-generation models\n- Multi-turn attacks can achieve higher success rates than single-turn approaches\n\n### Benchmark Analysis\n\nThe RedCode benchmark [2501.01234] provides a structured framework for evaluating code agent safety. Our analysis of benchmark results reveals several important insights:\n\n- There is significant variation in safety performance across different code agent implementations\n- Code generation and code execution safety are correlated but distinct dimensions\n- Agents with more permissive safety policies tend to achieve higher utility but lower safety scores\n\n### Trustworthiness Dimensions\n\nApplying the DecodingTrust framework [2306.11698] to code agents reveals trustworthiness concerns beyond traditional security metrics. Key dimensions include:\n\n1. **Reliability**: Consistency of code generation and execution behavior\n2. **Transparency**: Ability to explain code generation decisions and execution rationale\n3. **Fairness**: Absence of bias in code generation that could disadvantage certain groups\n4. **Privacy**: Protection of sensitive information in training data and user prompts\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe deployment of code agents in production environments requires rigorous safety evaluation through red-teaming methodologies. This paper has surveyed recent advances in code agent technology and red-teaming frameworks, highlighting the critical importance of comprehensive safety evaluation.\n\nOur analysis demonstrates that while code agents offer tremendous utility, they introduce novel security challenges that require specialized red-teaming approaches. The frameworks discussed in this work—including RedCodeAgent [2510.02609], RedCode [2501.01234], and DecodingTrust [2306.11698]—provide essential tools for evaluating and improving code agent safety.\n\nFuture research directions include:\n1. Developing more sophisticated automated red-teaming techniques\n2. Creating standardized benchmarks for code agent safety evaluation\n3. Investigating the trade-offs between code agent utility and safety\n4. Designing inherent safety mechanisms in code agent architectures\n\nAs code agents continue to advance in capability, the development of robust red-teaming methodologies will be essential for ensuring their safe and trustworthy deployment in real-world applications.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chulin Xie, Chengquan Guo, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chulin Xie, Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\nPAPER_EOF"
